 of a completely  polarizing protagonist duo it's  perfectly crafted story structure and  finally the immense  of realism and research that how to eat  she has put into his work to make the  story feel as incredibly convincing and  intriguing as it is so let's start by  taking a closer look at the two heroes  of our story first of all before we get  into the two protagonists I want to  mention that I believe IQ has a very  strong character set up in general over  the course of the story we increasingly  meet new teams with new characters and  philosophies and somehow most if not all  of them are immensely well-developed and  easy to connect with that being said  let's move on to our heroes the main  characters in high Q are the small but  hard-working Hinata show yo as well as  the talented but socially awkward  Kageyama Toby o strictly speaking the  story focuses more on Hinata as he  follows the road of the typical Shonen  hero  however Kageyama comes very close and  importance and enjoys a similar amount  of screen time what makes this quite  unlikely duo such a fantastic premise  for the story however is exactly that  they are the exact opposite of each  other Hinata is incredibly charismatic  and easily forms relationships with  teammates and opponents alike his  biggest weakness are his lack in height  as well as his inexperience that he has  to overcome through hard work and  training  Kageyama on the other hand has been  blessed with immense talent and instinct  for the sport and is one of the most  promising young prodigies in the country  however he likes basic social skills  that isolate him from his teammates as a  result he not an Kageyama have  completely opposing character arts where  one has to become better and stronger on  the court while the other has to become  a better person and learn how to form  real friendships two very popular themes  of Shonen that how to eg splits into two  characters here  what both have in common however is  their strong will to fight and work hard  in order to grow so in essence high Q is  about these two ambitious young men with  opposing strengths and weaknesses that  strives to become the best volleyball  players in the country by learning  from each other and growing together one  striving to become stronger and want to  become a better person the lesson here  is that one can be successful without  the other  and this makes both of them immensely  easy to identify with and makes us once  you see both of them succeed now the  next thing we have to talk about is high  Q's crazily perfected story structure  one of the most important criteria for a  great Shonen story at least according to  hirohiko Araki the author of JoJo's is  that the protagonists are always rising  rising and yeah you guessed it rising  again but the reason why an always  rising arc is so important is that we  always want to see the character keep  progressing further even if he has a  setbacks and low points the overall  development always points upwards  towards being a bit better than the last  chapter to better visualize this point  imagine a manga where the hero is weak  and friendless at the beginning nothing  too uncommon right however if he never  gets any better no matter how hard he  tries it would be kind of depressing to  keep watching this person's eternal  struggle right no what we want to see in  Shonen is how our heroes succeed and  grow and finally manage to be that one  annoyingly arrogant guy there always is  an annoyingly arrogant guy as you might  have guessed s' high Q has brought this  always rising structure to absolute  perfection the story starts with Hinata  being the only person on his volleyball  team during middle school not even  having the chance to have proper  practice but having to force his friends  to toss in balls from there the story  progresses from finally joining karasuno  high the former school of his idol /  playing at the inter high than the  spring high all the way to the Tokyo  Nationals despite having to face  progressively harder challenges along  the way  our heroes prevail in the end and keep  fighting and growing this gives the  entire series a sense of continuous  positive motion that we all want to be a  part of  the reason that not more anime can pull  off the tactic of the always rising arc  by using tournaments is what do you do  once the tournament is over and how do  you come up with increasingly stronger  and stronger opponents for the heroes  without becoming too repetitive haikyuu  solves these two quite common problems  by ending the series once a true high  point has been reached and more  importantly maybe is that Haru Ichi lets  his antagonists grow as well and have  them be a natural part of the journey  rival teams like nekoma or Alba juice I  grow alongside the car Asano team and  additionally we might have opponents  that might not be as strong as core  Asano  but have unique tactics or a unique  setup with which they can pose a true  threat to Hinata and Kageyama everything  in the story's development feels organic  and that already brings me to my final  points setting so what's so super  exciting about the setting in hai cube I  mean it's pretty much our normal world  right we have Japanese high schools with  teams that play volleyball in  tournaments no crazy magical powers no  furries no nothing well let me tell you  that it's actually exactly the  seamlessness of the Heike world that  makes it so good to watch the level of  realism and naturalness that how Ichi  creates is what fully emerges the reader  so us into the story and that's what  sparks this level of passion for the  sport he has without any doubt sunk  hours over hours into research about his  story from the basic rules and  techniques of the game over the  structures of Japanese high school  volleyball all the way to the history  and organization of the club's  themselves this includes pretty mundane  stuff you probably never thought about  like how many referees are there at each  tournaments or how do the uniforms have  to look or how does the sports club  actually finance itself all these things  are just there and make it easy for you  to lose yourself in the story but if  these things hadn't been thought about  properly the plot would surely have been  very different  can you imagine watching a sports anime  where the techniques aren't correct  you'll always know something is kind of  off and especially so if you actually  like the sport itself so I guess my  point here is for amazing anime the  devil truly lies in the detail and  haikyuu does a damn good job at it  so good even that professional player  Kim Jung kun praised the accuracy of the  anime in a review on her personal  YouTube channel noting that the creator  of the series must be a person with high  level of volleyball knowledge so if a  pro is able to enjoy the show to its  fullest it's no real surprise that we  can as well  so to recap what makes high q-- standout  among so many others shown in anime  today it has an incredibly intriguing  and fun protagonists duo it has a  constantly rising story structure in  which we see Hinata  Kageyama and really almost everyone else  in the show work incredibly hard to  become better players and better people  which let's face it is just ridiculously  entertaining to watch and finally the  story world is researched and created in  so much detail and with so much  dedication that it's really hard not to  become involved in it these three extra  ordinarily well-executed things are what  make haikyuu a true top Shonen of the  new era and goddamn now I really want to  go out and play but you know you can't  so that's it for today guys let me know  what you think about haikyuu and what  other shows I should talk about on this  channel if you enjoyed this video leave  me a thumbs up and if you want to hear  more of me over analyzing anime manga  make sure to hit that subscribe button  with 